## Explanation
A `SIGINT` (short for "Signal Interrupt") is a message sent by the operating system to your program, usually triggered when you press `Ctrl+C` on your keyboard. Think of it like a fire alarm in a building: normally, you are busy working (the program is running), but when the alarm rings (the `SIGINT` signal), you are forced to stop what you are doing to perform a specific "evacuation" procedure before leaving. In C, you can write a special function called a "signal handler" that tells your program, "When you receive this specific alarm, don't just quit immediately; run this cleanup code first."

## Use Cases
This is used when you want your program to exit gracefully rather than crashing. For example, if your program is writing data to a file, receiving a `SIGINT` allows the program to save the file and close it properly before shutting down, preventing data loss. It is also used to free up memory or close network connections that would otherwise stay "hanging" if the program were killed abruptly.

## Visual Model
```mermaid
